---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/religious-studies/intermediate/judaism">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an intermediate Religious Studies lesson. It assumes the foundational
    <em>The World's Traditions</em> lesson — you have met the major traditions and the
    shared vocabulary of sacred text, ritual and monotheism — and the intermediate
    <em>Studying Religion Academically</em> lesson, which explained why traditions are
    described from the inside, in their own terms. Here that method is applied to the
    oldest of the monotheisms, in its own categories.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>define Judaism as a covenant of practice and peoplehood rather than a creed;</li>
    <li>outline the three structuring covenants — Abraham, Sinai, David — and what each contributes;</li>
    <li>explain the double Torah, the Talmud's argumentative method, and halakhah;</li>
    <li>sketch the history from temple to text to diaspora, naming the thread of antisemitism honestly;</li>
    <li>distinguish the modern streams — Orthodox, Conservative, Reform — by where they locate authority.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Judaism is <strong>the covenant tradition: a roughly 3,000-year relationship
    between a people and God, lived through law, text and argument</strong>. It is
    the smallest of the major religions (about 15 million people, roughly 0.2 per
    cent of humanity) and one of the most consequential: it is the ancestor —
    scriptural, theological, and ethical — of both Christianity and Islam, and
    its modern shape was forged by catastrophe and adaptation. Temple to text
    after 70 CE; diaspora communities from Córdoba to Baghdad to Brooklyn; and a
    distinctive method — endless reasoned debate on sacred text — that shaped its
    people's intellectual culture as much as any creed.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="covenant, text, law, and the streams" widget="equationStep" config={judaCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four panels and watch the tradition's
    centre of gravity move — from covenant (relationship and obligation rather than creed),
    to text (the Temple's fall forcing the rebuild around books), to law as a total way of
    life, to the modern streams arguing over authority. Each panel depends on the one
    before: the peoplehood fact is what makes "one people, many streams" possible at all.
  </p>

  <h2>God's nature: the Shema and its disciplines</h2>
  <p>
    The line every Jewish service opens with is also the tradition's core claim:
    <strong>"Hear, O Israel: the LORD our God, the LORD is one"</strong>
    (Deuteronomy 6:4, the <em>Shema</em>). Jewish monotheism is strict: one
    God — creator of everything, sovereign of history, who cannot be depicted
    (the second commandment's ban on images is itself theology: God is not a
    thing among things) and whose name is treated with such reverence that it is
    not pronounced — the four-letter name YHWH is read as <em>Adonai</em>
    ("Lord") or, outside prayer, as <em>HaShem</em> ("the Name"); the
    scholarly convention of writing "G-d" performs the same deference. God is
    both <strong>transcendent</strong> (wholly other than creation) and
    <strong>immanent</strong> (present in history and relationship — walking
    with Adam, arguing with Abraham over Sodom: "Shall not the Judge of all the
    earth do justly?"). The classical attributes — omnipotent, omniscient,
    omnibenevolent, and in covenant with a particular people — generate the
    tradition's own hard problems (theodicy after catastrophe is not an
    abstraction here; post-Holocaust theology is a live subfield), and Jewish
    thought is unusually comfortable holding God to argument: Job receives no
    explanation, but the tradition has kept the argument running for millennia.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="fill the name and the grammar" widget="cloze" config={judNamesCloze} />

  <h2>Covenant: Abraham, Sinai, David</h2>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> Judaism centres on a creed — a list of beliefs
    one must assent to. It centres on a <strong>covenant</strong> (<em>brit</em>):
    a binding relationship of mutual obligation, and belonging is carried by
    practice and peoplehood more than doctrinal assent. Three covenants structure
    the story. <strong>Abraham</strong> (Genesis 12 and 17): God chooses one man's
    family — "I will make of you a great nation... and in you all the families
    of the earth shall be blessed" — sealed by circumcision; election is
    vocation, not privilege, a point rabbinic texts stress ("chosen for extra
    obligation"). <strong>Sinai</strong> (Exodus 19–20): the decisive one — the
    whole people, freed from Egypt, stands at the mountain and accepts the Torah;
    the midrash imagines the covenant offered to every nation first (each
    declining) and accepted by Israel — and later tradition extends it to all
    Jewish souls, past and future, standing at Sinai. The 613 <strong>mitzvot</strong>
    (commandments) of rabbinic counting are the covenant's terms: a total way of
    life, not a section of it. <strong>David</strong> (2 Samuel 7): God's promise
    of an eternal Davidic kingship — the covenant whose broken appearance drives
    the <strong>messianic hope</strong>: a future anointed king, personal or
    (in many modern readings) era, who restores Israel and perfects the world.
    <strong>Chosenness</strong> — the recurring accusation and the recurring
    self-question — functions inside the tradition as assignment of duty
    ("a light unto the nations"), not superiority; the tradition's own texts
    argue both directions, as usual.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="file each element under its covenant" widget="sort-buckets" config={judCovenantSort} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> covenant, not creed, is the centre — a binding relationship of
    mutual obligation whose terms are the mitzvot, with chosenness functioning as duty
    rather than privilege. Next, the texts that carry and interpret those terms.
  </p>

  <h2>Torah: written, oral, and endlessly interpreted</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Torah</strong> means "teaching" and names the Five Books of Moses
    (Genesis to Deuteronomy) — hand-written on parchment scrolls, read publicly
    through the annual cycle — but rabbinic Judaism holds a <strong>double
    Torah</strong>: the written Torah plus the <em>oral</em> Torah — the
    interpretation transmitted alongside it and compiled after the Temple's
    destruction into the <strong>Mishnah</strong> (c. 200 CE, Judah the Prince)
    and the <strong>Talmud</strong> (c. 500 CE for the Babylonian version) — the
    vast, multi-generational discussion of law and lore organised as commentary
    upon commentary, preserving majority <em>and</em> minority views on its
    pages. The famous Talmudic story of the oven of Akhnai makes the method
    doctrine: when Rabbi Eliezer calls on heaven to validate his ruling — and a
    carob tree uproots itself, a stream flows backward, a divine voice
    confirms him — the rabbis overrule God Himself: "It is not in heaven"
    (Deuteronomy 30:12); the Torah was given to humans to interpret. God, the
    story ends, laughed: "My children have defeated me." <strong>Interpretation
    is the tradition's main event.</strong> The classical fourfold scheme
    (<em>PaRDeS</em>: peshat — plain sense; remez — hints; derash — homiletical
    inquiry; sod — mystical secret) names the layers; <em>midrash</em> mines
    narrative gaps; and <strong>halakhah</strong> ("the way one walks") is the
    living legal system applying the texts to every domain — food
    (<strong>kashrut</strong>: permitted and forbidden species, the
    separation of meat and milk), time (the <strong>Sabbath</strong>: twenty-five
    hours of rest that switch the week's creating off), family, commerce. How
    binding all this is divides the modern streams.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="the oven of Akhnai — you are one of the rabbis" widget="branch" config={judAkhnaiBranch} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> kosher food is food a rabbi has blessed. No blessing makes food kosher: the rules concern species (no pork, no shellfish), slaughter, and the separation of meat from milk — a rabbi or certifying agency merely attests that the process was followed. Kashrut is a category of ritual fitness, not a health code and not a matter of magic words.</p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "Torah" means the law code — and observance
    means mechanical rule-following. Torah is teaching, narrative, argument and
    relationship; halakhah is case-law reasoning in a two-millennia debate; and
    the tradition's own texts treat study of the argument itself as worship —
    in some tellings, the reason the world was created.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="true or false — Torah vs law code" widget="judge" config={judTorahJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the double Torah and its endless interpretation — Mishnah,
    Talmud, midrash, halakhah — made study itself a form of worship and made a portable
    book the tradition's centre. Now trace the history that made that portability
    necessary.
  </p>

  <h2>History: temple to text to diaspora</h2>
  <p>
    The arc that the texts and the archaeology jointly sketch: ancestral
    traditions in Canaan; Israel attested externally from the
    <strong>Merneptah Stele</strong> (c. 1208 BCE — the first extra-biblical
    mention, an Egyptian victory inscription listing "Israel" among defeated
    peoples); monarchies (David and Solomon's united kingdom, per the texts, in
    the tenth century), the <strong>First Temple</strong> in Jerusalem, its
    destruction and the <strong>Babylonian exile</strong> (586 BCE) — from
    which Judaism returned edited, monotheised, and textual: the exile's
    template. The <strong>Second Temple</strong> period (516 BCE–70 CE) spans
    Greek conquest, the Maccabean revolt (the Chanukah story), Roman rule, and
    the sects the Dead Sea Scrolls illuminate. Then the decisive catastrophe:
    Rome crushed two revolts, destroyed the Temple (70 CE), and ended the
    sacrificial religion Judaism had been. What survived was <strong>rabbinic
    Judaism</strong> — rebuilt at Yavneh around text, prayer and law, portable
    because its centre was a book. The <strong>diaspora</strong> (from Greek:
    scattering) organised into great centres: <strong>Sephardi</strong> (Iberia —
    a golden age of Hebrew poetry and philosophy under Islam, ended by the 1492
    expulsion), <strong>Mizrahi</strong> (the Middle Eastern communities —
    Baghdad, Cairo, Persia — continuous since Babylonian times),
    <strong>Ashkenazi</strong> (Europe — Yiddish-speaking, carrying both the
    medieval persecutions and, from the eighteenth century, <strong>Hasidism</strong>:
    the mystical revival founded around the Baal Shem Tov, teaching that joy,
    song and devotion can outrank scholarly rank). Emancipation (full citizenship,
    from late-eighteenth-century Europe onward) then posed the modern question —
    can you be a citizen of a nation-state and a member of an ancient people? —
    that produced the modern denominations and, catastrophically, the backlash:
    modern antisemitism culminating in the <strong>Holocaust</strong>
    (<em>Shoah</em>) — six million Jews murdered, two-thirds of European Jewry —
    after which the state of <strong>Israel</strong> (1948) changed the
    tradition's demography and its theology's problems.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="scrub the arc — temple to text to diaspora" widget="timeline" config={judArcTimeline} />
  <p>
    Any honest account must also name the thread running through it:
    <strong>antisemitism</strong> — from the church's early supersessionism (the
    claim that Christianity replaced Israel, which decoded the Torah's people
    as obsolete), through medieval expulsions (England 1290, France 1306, Spain
    1492), ghettoisation, blood-libel and the pogroms of the Russian Empire, to
    the industrialised murder of the Holocaust. The study of Judaism therefore
    includes the study of its persecutions — but the tradition's own account of
    itself refuses to be defined by them: the same two millennia produced the
    Talmud, Hebrew poetry, mysticism, and the reinventions of every exile.
    Survival through adaptation — a text-centred, portable peoplehood — is
    arguably one of history's most successful survival strategies, and the
    argument over what was preserved and what was lost is a central one in
    contemporary Jewish studies.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="drag between the thread and the creations" widget="compare" config={judThreadCompare} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> from the First Temple's fall to the diaspora's great centres,
    the pattern is catastrophe answered by adaptation — and the modern denominations are
    the latest answers to emancipation's question. Their differences are sharpest on one
    point: authority.
  </p>

  <h2>One people, many streams</h2>
  <p>
    Modern Judaism's denominations are answers to modernity, and their
    differences are sharpest on authority. <strong>Orthodox</strong> (both the
    modern-Orthodox engagement with secular culture and the stricter
    <em>Haredi</em> world): the double Torah is revealed and halakhah is
    binding. <strong>Conservative</strong> (Masorti): accepts historical-critical
    scholarship but keeps halakhic process authoritative-ish — change through
    law, not against it. <strong>Reform</strong>: personal autonomy in
    observance, progressive revelation, ethics over ritual — the largest North
    American stream. Plus <strong>Reconstructionist</strong> (Judaism as evolving
    civilisation) and <strong>secular-humanist</strong> forms. Beneath the
    denominations runs the peoplehood fact that makes Judaism structurally
    different from its daughters: Jewish identity is carried by birth as well as
    belief — a Jew who practises nothing is, by halakhic and communal
    definition, still a Jew. Religion and ethnicity intertwine; the traditions'
    own category for itself is a nation with a covenant. Lifecycle practice
    encodes it: circumcision at eight days, <strong>bar/bat mitzvah</strong>
    (taking on the commandments at 13 — the moment a child becomes responsible
    for the covenant), marriage under a <em>chuppah</em>, burial and the
    <em>kaddish</em> year of mourning — and the festivals turn the national
    memory into an annual body-practice: <strong>Passover</strong>'s seder table
    ("in every generation..."), the High Holy Days and <strong>Yom Kippur</strong>'s
    collective audit of the soul. And <strong>Shabbat</strong> — the weekly
    twenty-five-hour Sabbath — is the tradition's portable temple: candles,
    two loaves, no work from sundown Friday to nightfall Saturday, a covenant
    sign (the rabbis say) that observant families have carried through every
    exile because it repeats every week wherever they are. Jewish ethics
    rides the same structure: <em>tzedakah</em> (righteous giving — not
    charity but justice, ideally structured as dignity), the Torah's
    repeated grounding of law in memory of slavery ("love the stranger,
    for you were strangers in Egypt").
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="pair each stream with where it locates authority" widget="match" config={judStreamsMatch} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>What centres Judaism if not a creed, and what do the 613 mitzvot represent?</li>
    <li>What changed in 70 CE, and what did rabbinic Judaism rebuild around?</li>
    <li>Why is "kosher means blessed by a rabbi" wrong — what actually makes food kosher?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) the covenant (brit) — a binding relationship of mutual
    obligation; the mitzvot are the covenant's terms, a total way of life rather than one
    section of it. (2) Rome destroyed the Second Temple and ended the sacrificial religion;
    rabbinic Judaism rebuilt at Yavneh around text, prayer and law — portable because its
    centre was a book. (3) the rules concern species, slaughter and the separation of meat
    and milk; a rabbi or certifying agency merely attests that the process was followed —
    kashrut is a category of ritual fitness, not a matter of magic words.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Judaism is a roughly 3,000-year covenant between a people and God, carried by practice and peoplehood more than doctrinal assent.</li>
    <li>Abraham, Sinai and David are the structuring covenants; Sinai is the decisive one, and election means vocation, not privilege.</li>
    <li>The double Torah — written plus oral — makes interpretation the tradition's main event.</li>
    <li>Halakhah is the walked path: law covering every domain, argued case by case across two millennia.</li>
    <li>After 70 CE the tradition rebuilt from temple to text — the portability that made diaspora survival possible.</li>
    <li>Antisemitism runs through the whole history, yet the tradition's own account refuses to be defined by persecution.</li>
    <li>The modern streams differ chiefly on authority: Orthodox binding halakhah, Reform personal autonomy.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The two daughter traditions that inherited the scriptural and ethical architecture
    described here are treated in the intermediate <em>Christianity</em> and <em>Islam</em>
    lessons, and <em>Studying Religion Academically</em> returns to the method questions
    this page has been practising — how to describe a tradition in its own terms without
    simply adopting them.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Four extensions. <strong>Negative theology</strong>: Maimonides (1138–1204,
    <em>Guide for the Perplexed</em>) argued that positive claims about God
    misfire — we know what God is not — a rigorously philosophical Judaism that
    read Greek philosophy as covenant commentary; his thirteen principles became
    the closest Judaism came to a creed. <strong>Kabbalah</strong>: the mystical
    tradition (Zohar, thirteenth-century Spain; Lurianic Safed, sixteenth
    century) whose audacious move — <em>tzimtzum</em>, God contracting the
    infinite self to make room for a world — rethought creation, exile and
    repair (<em>tikkun olam</em>, a term now secularised into universal
    charity-speak) and whose pop afterlife (Madonna's study circle) is not what
    its redactors intended. <strong>The Talmud's intellectual afterlife</strong>:
    the argumentative pedagogy — challenge, counterexample, distinction,
    precedent — is a real candidate for explaining the outsized contributions
    of Talmud-literate populations to law, science and economics after
    emancipation; the mechanism (argument as sacred play) is at least as
    plausible as the genes it used to be credited to. And the <strong>open
    questions</strong>: Israel–diaspora tensions over who speaks for Judaism;
    intermarriage, patrilineal descent (American Reform accepts, Orthodox
    does not) and the demographic experiments of American Jewry; and the
    post-Holocaust theological problem — "after Auschwitz", argued between
    Fackenheim's commandment not to hand Hitler posthumous victories and
    Rubenstein's death-of-God radicalism — still the tradition's hardest
    case.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> the Mishnah and Talmud (translated editions); Holtz, Back to the Sources; Maimonides, Guide for the Perplexed; the Merneptah Stele (Egyptian Museum, Cairo). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
